(3b) THEOREM SG1-1:  (i) covariance  AND  (ii) F<=s1  AND  finite-moment computability = EMPTY  [T]
============================================================================================
  proof by the atom family. For any a>0 put  nu_eps^a = (eps/2lam) delta_eps + (1-eps/2lam) delta_a .
  eps -> 0 : the moment vector converges to that of delta_a ,  but s1(nu_eps^a) = eps -> 0 .
  (ii) for all eps  =>  F(delta_a) <= 0 .   (i) on atoms =>  F(delta_a) = F(delta_0) + a .
  => F(delta_0) + a <= 0  for ALL a > 0  =>  contradiction.  qed

         a    s1(nu_eps^a) @eps=1e-6    |dm1| vs delta_a (ii) forces F<=   (i) forces F=   consistent?
       0.5                    1.0e-6          2.49999e-7          1.0e-6             0.5            NO
       1.0                    1.0e-6          4.99999e-7          1.0e-6             1.0            NO
       2.0                    1.0e-6          9.99999e-7          1.0e-6             2.0            NO
       5.0                    1.0e-6              2.5e-6          1.0e-6             5.0            NO
  => no F can satisfy both sides.  The only escape is to give up finite-moment computability.
     Full-moment sequence DOES determine nu (compact support => unique measure) => the unique (i)+(ii) object
     reachable that way is s1 itself => G = 0.  Finite truncation is exactly what the atom family kills.
